diagnosis_text:
A comprehensive multi-phase diagnostic pass across all 28 devices found no network fault. The reported symptom — pc1 (10.5.0.1) unable to reach the 172.16.30.x group while other Campus 1 subnets are reachable — is not reproducible in the current live state.

Direct evidence: a 4-packet ICMP probe from pc1 to 172.16.30.12 returns 0% loss at 2.19 ms average. Both sshswitch and router17 carry a valid, FIB-selected OSPF route for 172.16.30.0/24 (cost 45 and 40, respectively). FRR running-configs on all five inter-campus routers (mswitch0, router16, router7, router17, sshswitch) contain only plain OSPF `network` statements — no distribute-lists, prefix-lists, or route-maps that could selectively suppress the 172.16.30.0/24 advertisement. infra_sweep found no ACL/nftables rules and confirmed all 15 hosts have IPv4 addresses and default routes. l2_snapshot found no duplicate MACs. pressure_sweep found no flap scripts or resource stress. The ospf_snapshot confirms all nine routers are fully converged with identical route counts (18 each) and Full-state adjacencies.

The route-age timestamps on sshswitch indicate OSPF learned all remote prefixes approximately 55 seconds after interface bring-up, which is within normal OSPF Hello/Dead-timer convergence. There is no selective mechanism that would have blocked only 172.16.30.0/24 while leaving 172.16.10.0/24 and 172.16.20.0/24 reachable. Without a historical trace, log entry, or flap mechanism, the user's report cannot be corroborated and is classified as unconfirmed.
=== END ===